[{:db/id [:question/math-academy-id "q-96513"] :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14061054/assets/d3a06bdc7a63a86651752ff70331010920d24728e7f4e4ad9f067eb860ebaf4b.png)\n\nConsider the phase line shown above. Which of the following differential equations could have this phase line?\n\n- $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}={y}^{2}-1$\n- $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=(y-1)^{2}(y+1)$\n- $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=(y-1)^{2}$" :question/worked-solution "First, notice that all three differential equations are of the form\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=f(y).\n$$\n\nThe phase line gives us the following information:\n\n- The equilibrium solutions are $y=-1$ and $y=1.$\n\n- $f(y)$ is positive for $y\\in (-\\infty ,-1)\\cup (1,\\infty ).$\n\n- $f(y)$ is negative for $y\\in (-1,1).$\n\nWith this in mind, let's study each of the given equations.\n\n- We start by considering ${y}^{′}={y}^{2}-1.$\n\nFirst, we see that $f(y)={y}^{2}-1,$ so $f(y)=0$ if $y=-1,1.$ So, the equilibrium solutions correspond to those shown in the phase line.\n\nNow, we can construct a sign table for $f(y)$ as follows: \n\n|  | $(-\\infty ,-1)$ | $(-1,1)$ | $(1,\\infty )$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $y+1$ | $-$ | $+$ | $+$ |\n| $y-1$ | $-$ | $-$ | $+$ |\n| $f(y)$ | $+$ | $-$ | $+$ |\n\nSo, we have that $f(y)$ is negative for  $y\\in (-1,1),$ and $f(y)$ is positive for $y\\in (-\\infty ,-1)\\cup (1,\\infty ).$ This corresponds to the arrows shown on the phase line.\n\nTherefore, equation I has the given phase line.\n\n- Next, we consider ${y}^{′}=(y-1)^{2}(y+1).$\n\nFirst, $f(y)=(y-1)^{2}(y+1),$ so $f(y)=0$ if $y=-1,1.$ So, the equilibrium solutions correspond to those shown in the phase line.\n\nHowever, we note that $f(y)<0$ for $y\\in (-\\infty ,-1).$ This does *not* correspond to the arrows shown on the phase line.\n\nTherefore, equation II does not have the given phase line.\n\n- Finally, we consider ${y}^{′}=(y-1)^{2}.$\n\nIn this case, $f(y)=(y-1)^{2},$ and the equilibrium solution is $y=1$ only.\n\nTherefore, equation III does not have the given phase line.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\"" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:question/math-academy-id "q-96516"] :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14061054/assets/b958bd4db4353cfe7a852b79f7144d0b48b85c48b3d97f813b09db2858ec110a.png)\n\nConsider the differential equation $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=f(y),$ where the graph of $f(y)$ is shown above. Which of the following is the phase line corresponding to this differential equation?" :question/worked-solution "From the graph of the function $f(y),$ we can deduce the following information:\n\n- The solutions to $f(y)=0$ are $y=-3,y=0$ and $y=3.$ Therefore $y=-3,0,3$ are the equilibrium solutions of the differential equation.\n\n- $f(y)>0$ when $y\\in (-3,0)\\cup (3,\\infty ).$\n\n- $f(y)<0$ when $y\\in (-\\infty ,-3)\\cup (0,3).$\n\nTherefore, we can draw the phase line using the following recipe:\n\n- Draw a vertical $y$-axis\n\n- Mark the equilibrium solutions\n\n- Draw arrows pointing up in the intervals where $f(y)>0$\n\n- Draw arrows pointing down in the intervals where $f(y)<0$\n\nApplying the above recipe, we get the following phase line for our differential equation.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14061054/assets/666ae97e41e825161c68cc6146a06c70b87c6d84b11ae8116c0d415c340987fa.png)" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "q-97915/selection/304a2a253b70b3735e03216e1354fed72fcf23d610f94c83110b1603a38b3c79" :answer/id #uuid "ac0bb362-7aa5-567b-863a-70f246ba8470" :answer/type :answer.type/image :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14061054/assets/78373cbb2e323dcbfbc40770fb47a59939670ca8e27a70b3ef19eaa04ba3f939.png" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-97915/selection/4c0fafc274415d5b2c177ffb223eb71760e11acd00940ab089175b3652d63ff8" :answer/id #uuid "9dd8ec8d-cbfa-51d1-b0aa-9aff0445243a" :answer/type :answer.type/image :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14061054/assets/2b5796afbbcf6ec35a0980a82957ddbf74813b0113b621a0b77cfc24f8706838.png" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-97915/selection/024327858d57b92953d38505f2b67afd71ae59cdb8f0448492e366959822b3cc" :answer/id #uuid "77a44e6b-d2f8-5484-9cde-1ed8a7547b5b" :answer/type :answer.type/image :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14061054/assets/73994f96f68cd43ee0377ea2005479e0c927e37dc0a15e2e9da35c1c8900afa0.png" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-97915/selection/cffe7f309336b86471419bf305cf05e0fdff6bc8b28e853f3997f6113dfbe94c" :answer/id #uuid "fe7f20b9-f7b9-5fbf-bbac-f7cf8d5048df" :answer/type :answer.type/image :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14061054/assets/e17edbcca21405dde82e9b87ad0f870fa3e4383572785210d5b63cc706f57897.png" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-97915/selection/9704b13055b75d8103e068b5500e3b656d97a8b6f64de35717643610c0584f1b" :answer/id #uuid "83108880-be61-5974-b590-83d266bc5024" :answer/type :answer.type/image :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14061054/assets/2b51f096104fe981a341096c945881595529d17061d0e5656e1ef3d1a9f79df4.png" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-97915/selection" :answer-field/id #uuid "165d44d5-4225-5c4d-b377-bfddc521721c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "q-97915/selection/9704b13055b75d8103e068b5500e