n/answer-fields ["q-97915/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "36070a0f-0b40-5fa6-9357-9f0c754b00a4"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-97915"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-98040/selection/d9a9e0debbbd4a006b71472ce29106db6e9ae726449bf23334da8c675c19de9b" :answer/id #uuid "1d62cd27-e989-5d8e-84ab-cc994623c357" :answer/type :answer.type/image :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14061054/assets/f22dc056accfeae38eea6fb0207adfe4676bd8bd875cbeb96c078f4eb6457a6e.png" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-98040/selection/b95754b57984bf496760008be03cb0e126409889da3a36372282d64e3f6065bd" :answer/id #uuid "7d5386a3-bea2-5aaf-acca-f6f751e65352" :answer/type :answer.type/image :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14061054/assets/f95aa207248151fc7035fcd6dcdea8de58ebff368aa886b18413249bf30225ba.png" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-98040/selection/0fd593da89d487a40f1d8a6210a78efd691124f5fcdd552cabb41e0a327d0a9a" :answer/id #uuid "093b88c7-ea2c-58fd-a8b2-bde1e7dee6cd" :answer/type :answer.type/image :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14061054/assets/d46036d0f5c0fc689d1123e3512a446b94688f6fa94df017ee26682860ed6b64.png" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-98040/selection/a062e4c1bf522f9370406326bc7ae43f033e1407307f4749ff9e6313f02f3120" :answer/id #uuid "3b41e1bd-5d3a-5001-90ed-633a34435d1f" :answer/type :answer.type/image :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14061054/assets/03d9436c922b602b3e3388dfc21b6e8c20aae96f70b378de706b3c96b7ded253.png" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-98040/selection/b04ef05e4eb96ab14f93e934dc594a529e3b59e24d12e2e0f7219d91d436fd34" :answer/id #uuid "ba82f68b-7f08-5823-8363-0993b3caaf7e" :answer/type :answer.type/image :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14061054/assets/86bcece5228ba30310e4b4ed663c5efcb551609474fa924034efac08aa055a23.png" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-98040/selection" :answer-field/id #uuid "13325c0c-2256-5a30-ba60-d90a721de45c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "q-98040/selection/d9a9e0debbbd4a006b71472ce29106db6e9ae726449bf23334da8c675c19de9b" :answer-field/choices ["q-98040/selection/d9a9e0debbbd4a006b71472ce29106db6e9ae726449bf23334da8c675c19de9b" "q-98040/selection/b95754b57984bf496760008be03cb0e126409889da3a36372282d64e3f6065bd" "q-98040/selection/0fd593da89d487a40f1d8a6210a78efd691124f5fcdd552cabb41e0a327d0a9a" "q-98040/selection/a062e4c1bf522f9370406326bc7ae43f033e1407307f4749ff9e6313f02f3120" "q-98040/selection/b04ef05e4eb96ab14f93e934dc594a529e3b59e24d12e2e0f7219d91d436fd34"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-98040" :question/id #uuid "4b7cd7a6-7b0b-599b-8ee5-35ee0fead25b" :question/math-academy-id "q-98040" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14061054/assets/58369eb9878aba12bf52d22a930ec348e7b6cf31e1b2c8d77823de1aab5d9c87.png)\n\nThe slope field of the differential equation $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=f(y)$ is shown above. Which of the following is the phase line of the given equation?" :question/worked-solution "From the slope field, we can deduce the following:\n\n- $y=-2$ and $y=0$ are equilibrium solutions.\n\n- For all $y\\in (0,\\infty ),$ the slopes of the slope field are negative. Therefore, $f(y)<0$ in this region.\n\n- For all $y\\in (-\\infty ,-2)\\cup (-2,0),$ the slopes of the slope field are positive. Therefore, $f(y)>0$ in this region.\n\nNow, we can draw the phase line using the following recipe:\n\n- Draw a vertical $y$-axis\n\n- Mark the equilibrium solutions\n\n- Draw arrows pointing up in the intervals where $f(y)>0$\n\n- Draw arrows pointing down in the intervals where $f(y)<0$\n\nApplying the above recipe, we get the following phase line for our differential equation.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14061054/assets/fcc9f998111493167284cf5844645ac2201a230b073a9336604fb790ba16bad4.png)" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["q-98040/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "c72cc6e0-deae-51fc-a5e4-b27fcb6f7892"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-98040"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-98632/selection/5b26512916f768e66a2b0af106e5aa3e2ff7ee012277d9669a26d349c343f77b" :answer/id #uuid "8a6e8826-3851-5ddc-ab3c-5ecdb6c30c63" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-98632/selection/6a87b4860f595a380edef797b2cc43b24d4896fc51da713259b980588721456c" :answer/id #uuid "c27fff79-8109-54bd-a3ee-63b85cc9ce83" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "III only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-98632/selection/df67a18079e3b7b726c109ae1bf07ae161d9f6d37a928c50361c8787d74f7981" :answer/id #uuid "e541cd39-0d40-55cf-91a7-09d8a31aeb06" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "II only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-98632/selection/95f63c11f99a5015c1f73558576052145e00c1cfbb1718f78cf554be0ab200f9" :answer/id #uuid "94e03541-5d84-5fdb-96c1-709122d1374d" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I and II only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-98632/selection/421ba3cf61c86c7d854403490925f53920cd3c015c12342698571d9a74aca2fd" :answer/id #uuid "57fe8823-70c4-5f0b-a359-9ad3fe8f4b98" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "None of the listed" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-98632/selection" :answer-field/id #uuid "a0769a07-0b20-52e2-b52d-b895a1561a9f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "q-98632/selection/6a87b4860f595a380edef797b2cc43b24d4896fc51da713259b980588721456c" :answer-field/choices ["q-98632/selection/5b26512916f768e66a2b0af106e5aa3e2ff7ee012277d9669a26d349c343f77b" "q-98632/selection/6a87b4860f595a380edef797b2cc43b24d4896fc51da713259b980588721456c" "q-98632/selection/df67a18079e3b7b726c109ae1bf07ae161d9f6d37a928c50361c8787d74f7981" "q-98632/selection/95f63c11f99a5015c1f73558576052145e00c1cfbb1718f78cf554be0ab200f9" "q-98632/selection/421ba3cf61c86c7d854403490925f53920cd3c015c12342698571d9a74aca2fd"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-98632" :question/id #uuid "b30c866d-fa3d-5692-b249-cd39aee84dde" :question/math-academy-id "q-98632" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14061054/assets/ca296c3a8a2989fd5e5ca647ad4f7d0f4890aa3caf8d9e0a1fce98498d2d7d64.png)\n\nConsider the phase line shown above. Which of the following differential equations could have this phase line?\n\n- $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=(y+1)^{2}$\n- $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=(y-2)^{2}(y+1)$\n- $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}={y}^{2}-y-2$" :question/worked-solution "First, notice that all three differential equations are of the form\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=f(y).\n$$\n\nThe phase line gives us the following information:\n\n- The equilibrium solutions are $y=-1$ and $y=2.$\n\n- $f(y)$ is positive for $y\\in (-\\infty ,-1)\\cup (2,\\infty ).$\n\n- $f(y)$ is negative for $y\\in (-1,2).$\n\nWith this in mind, let's study each of the given equations.\n\n- We start by considering ${y}^{′}=(y+1)^{2}.$\n\nIn this case, $f(y)=(y+1)^{2},$ and the equilibrium solution is $y=-1$ only.\n\nTherefore, equation I does not have the given phase line.\n\n- Next, we consider ${y}^{′}=(y-2)^{2}(y+1).$\n\nFirst, $f(y)=(y-2)^{2}(y+1),$ so $f(y)=0$ if $y=-1,2.$ So, the equilibrium solutions correspond to those shown in the phase line.\n\nHowever, we note that $f(y)<0$ for $y\\in (-\\infty ,-1).$ This does *not* correspond to the arrows shown on the phase line.\n\nTherefore, equation II does not have the given phase line.\n\n- Finally, we consider ${y}^{′}={y}^{2}-y-2.$\n\nFirst, we see that $f(y)=(y+1)(y-2),$ so $f(y)=0$ if $y=-1,2.$ So, the equilibrium solutions correspond to those shown in the phase line.\n\nNow, we can construct a sign table for $f(y)$ as follows: \n\n|  | $(-\\infty ,-1)$ | $(-1,2)$ | $(2,\\infty )$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $y+1$ | $-$ | $+$ | $+$ |\n| $y-2$ | $-$ | $-$ | $+$ |\n| $f(y)$ | $+$ | $-$ | $+$ |\n\nSo, we have that $f(y)$ is negative for  $y\\in (-1,2),$ and $f(y)$ is positive for $y\\in (-\\infty ,-1)\\cup (2,\\infty ).$ This corresponds to the arrows shown on the phase line.\n\nTherefore, equation III has the given phase line.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only.\"" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["q-98632/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "6e71c0c9-7dd1-5f09-a55c-96b5bed1ee9a"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-98632"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]}]
